[
    {
        "content": "<p>Currently, mathlib has typeclasses for continuous actions (<a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ContinuousSMul#doc\">docs#ContinuousSMul</a> if the action is jointly continuous, <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ContinuousConstSMul#doc\">docs#ContinuousConstSMul</a> if continuous in each variable).<br>\nThere are no such classes for C^n actions yet; it is time to change this. (This is needed to formalise quotient manifolds and smooth principle bundles, for example.)<br>\n<a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/38527\">#38527</a> (for <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ContDiff#doc\">docs#ContDiff</a>) and <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/38565\">#38565</a> (for <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ContMDiff#doc\">docs#ContMDiff</a>) propose adding such actions.</p>\n<p>I'd like input on two related design questions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>do we want typeclasses for both ContDiff and ContMDiff? <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"646363\">@Ben Eltschig</span> has proposed yes <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/38527#pullrequestreview-4177197601\">comment</a>; I haven't thought about it deeply yet</li>\n<li>do we want both <code>ContMDiffSMul</code> and <code>ContMDiffConstSMul</code>? In the continuous case, we do since there are meaningful settings where the action is continuous in each variable, but not jointly continuous. I am not sure if this is the case here. If <code>G</code> acts smoothly on <code>M</code>, and both are finite-dimensional, being smooth in each argument <del>is</del> should be equivalent to being jointly smooth. (Do we have this in mathlib already, maybe in the normed space setting?) If both <code>G</code> and <code>M</code> were infinite-dimensional, these are almost surely not equivalent: but \"infinite-dimensional Lie groups\" are not a thing/are fairly exotic differential geometry (requiring convenient vector spaces or somesuch... I'm confident ignoring this until a need arises). A finite-dimensional Lie group acting on a Banach manifold <em>is</em> fairly standard; this should be covered in the definition we choose. I have a fairly strong hunch these are still equivalent (basically, the \"jointly smooth implies smooth\" argument is probably robust w.r.t. finite <em>co</em>dimension, not finite <em>overall dimension</em>); I haven't thought about a detailed proof.<br>\nDoes anybody know if these are equivalent? CC <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"214703\">@Yury G. Kudryashov</span> <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"110050\">@Sébastien Gouëzel</span> <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"110031\">@Patrick Massot</span> <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"260507\">@Heather Macbeth</span> <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"111080\">@Floris van Doorn</span> <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"240862\">@Oliver Nash</span></li>\n</ul>",
        "id": 593109344,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1778016856
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Do you have a reference / proof sketch of the result in finite dimensions? I actually haven't heard about it in that case either, and it isn't obvious to me</p>",
        "id": 593110046,
        "sender_full_name": "Ben Eltschig",
        "timestamp": 1778017214
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Perhaps this is my intuition going wrong! (See also <a href=\"https://math.stackexchange.com/a/2287465\">https://math.stackexchange.com/a/2287465</a>.)</p>",
        "id": 593110582,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1778017480
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yep, for general functions it definitely doesn't hold. It seems plausible to me that it could for group actions, since those are a lot more rigid; but I don't see why, and I haven't been able to find a counterexample either</p>",
        "id": 593110752,
        "sender_full_name": "Ben Eltschig",
        "timestamp": 1778017556
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>That's my thinking also.</p>",
        "id": 593110789,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1778017572
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Just so I don't forget later, this seems relevant: actions of topological groups on locally compact spaces are continuous iff they are continuous in each variable, <a href=\"https://mathoverflow.net/a/415698\">https://mathoverflow.net/a/415698</a>. I haven't yet thought deeply about whether this lets you prove smoothness in the finite-dimensional case or not, but it at least makes it more plausible that the result holds in finite but not infinite dimensions</p>",
        "id": 593122553,
        "sender_full_name": "Ben Eltschig",
        "timestamp": 1778024103
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't think <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ContinuousConstSMul#doc\">docs#ContinuousConstSMul</a> means continuous in each variable, it's only for continuity in the second variable. Anyway, I'd introduce the classes in the differentiable setting only when we need them. I'm sure <code>ContMDiffSMul</code> is relevant, I'm less sure for <code>ContMDiffConstSMul</code> because I don't have relevant examples in mind for now.</p>",
        "id": 593169194,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1778049086
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"646363\">Ben Eltschig</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Typeclasses.20for.20smooth.20actions.3A.20.60Cont.28M.29Diff.28Const.29SMul.60/near/593122553\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Just so I don't forget later, this seems relevant: actions of topological groups on locally compact spaces are continuous iff they are continuous in each variable, <a href=\"https://mathoverflow.net/a/415698\">https://mathoverflow.net/a/415698</a>. I haven't yet thought deeply about whether this lets you prove smoothness in the finite-dimensional case or not, but it at least makes it more plausible that the result holds in finite but not infinite dimensions</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes, the result does hold in the finite dimensional case, but the proof is nontrivial. I did some digging and found a reference in <em>Topological transformation groups</em>, Montgomery D., Zippin L., 1955, pg. 212-13.</p>\n<p><strong>Theorem.</strong>[Montgomery-Zippin] Suppose a Lie group <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span></span></span></span> acts continuously on a <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C^k</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> manifold <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">M</span></span></span></span> by <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mtext>•</mtext><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(g,x) = g • x</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">g</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">g</span><span class=\"mord\">•</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span></span></span></span>. If for each fixed <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g \\in G</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7335em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">g</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span></span></span></span>, the map <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>↦</mo><mi>g</mi><mtext>•</mtext><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x ↦ g • x</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.522em;vertical-align:-0.011em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">↦</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">g</span><span class=\"mord\">•</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span></span></span></span> is <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C^k</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>, then <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(g,x)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">g</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> is jointly <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C^k</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> in <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(g,x)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">g</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> .</p>\n<p>Combine with the Robert Ellis result you cited (<a href=\"https://mathoverflow.net/questions/415680/difference-between-definitions-of-continuous-action-profinite-case/415698#415698\">https://mathoverflow.net/questions/415680/difference-between-definitions-of-continuous-action-profinite-case/415698#415698</a>) we obtain:</p>\n<p><strong>Corollary.</strong> For a Lie group <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span></span></span></span> acting on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">M</span></span></span></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(g,x)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">g</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> is jointly <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C^k</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> whenever both:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>↦</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g ↦ f(x,g)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7054em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">g</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">↦</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">g</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> is continuous for any fixed <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x \\in X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5782em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>↦</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x ↦ f(x,g)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.522em;vertical-align:-0.011em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">↦</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">g</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> is <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C^k</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> for any fixed <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g \\in G</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7335em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">g</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span></span></span></span></li>\n</ol>",
        "id": 593178127,
        "sender_full_name": "Jack McCarthy",
        "timestamp": 1778052156
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks a lot for the reference! (Reminds me of my PhD thesis, when I also used some abstract Lie group result by them.) Does Montgomery-Zippin's proof generalise to Lie groups acting on Banach manifolds?</p>",
        "id": 593181098,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1778052989
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I agree with Sébastien: we should introduce those type class only when needed instead to preemptively cover all potential uses.</p>",
        "id": 593182298,
        "sender_full_name": "Patrick Massot",
        "timestamp": 1778053334
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Formalizing Montgomery-Zippin stuff would be nice, but I don’t think it will block anything at this stage, we can simply add assumptions, and maybe add to the documentation a note saying the assumption can actually be weakened. Concretely I really wouldn’t want holding up the basic theorems like quotients by smooth free proper actions on this.</p>",
        "id": 593182711,
        "sender_full_name": "Patrick Massot",
        "timestamp": 1778053464
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"634338\">Michael Rothgang</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Typeclasses.20for.20smooth.20actions.3A.20.60Cont.28M.29Diff.28Const.29SMul.60/near/593181098\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Does Montgomery-Zippin's proof generalise to Lie groups acting on Banach manifolds?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Unclear. From what I can tell, most of the argument is mostly based on studying 1-parameter families in <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span></span></span></span> and using the Baire Category Theorem on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span>, which all generalizes. However, there are a handful of coordinate computations, and in particular an application of Taylor's theorem which I'm not sure how you would generalize them to the Banach case. You'd probably need to add some additional assumptions to make it work, or invoke some more modern machinery.</p>",
        "id": 593185816,
        "sender_full_name": "Jack McCarthy",
        "timestamp": 1778054345
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This question came up again in <a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"541885\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/541885-ItaLean-2025/topic/Projects.3A.20Quotient.20Manifolds/with/609248903\">#ItaLean 2025 &gt; Projects: Quotient Manifolds</a>. The motivating question is not mathematical generality, but formalization convenience.</p>",
        "id": 609249328,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1783608347
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"646363\">Ben Eltschig</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/541885-ItaLean-2025/topic/Projects.3A.20Quotient.20Manifolds/near/609034748\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>are quotients of manifolds by discrete group actions an example [where this matters in practice] maybe? if you want to provide an <code>IsManifold</code> instance for the quotient space, the model with corners of it would be the same as that of the original manifold, right? So you could make it an instance, but only if you don't also take in a model with corners for the group?</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 609249339,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1783608349
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I agree with Ben's observation. I'm not sure yet if this is worth the effort of introducing the additional class. I'd be interested in seeing the code with the current class, to get a feeling how onerous it actually is.</p>",
        "id": 609249645,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1783608419
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Alright I will try to finish the proof here <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/40727\">#40727</a> using the <code>ContMDiffSMul</code> definition asap</p>",
        "id": 609254069,
        "sender_full_name": "Pepa Montero Jimena",
        "timestamp": 1783609485
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"110050\">@Sébastien Gouëzel</span> has convinced me that we should have <code>ContMDiffConstSMul</code>, for formalization reasons.</p>\n<p>If a discrete group acts by smooth maps (i.e. <code>ContMDiffConstSMul</code>), it also acts smoothly (<a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ContMDiffSMul#doc\">docs#ContMDiffSMul</a>) since a discrete topological space is a 0-dimensional manifold. However, the model with corners on such a space is not canonical (neither mathematically nor in Lean). We could choose <code>Unit</code> as model space with model <code>𝓘(𝕜, Unit)</code>, which might be the most natural in abstract cases. However, the product action of <code>G</code> and <code>H</code>, the product charted space would be on <code>G × H</code>, modelled on <code>Unit × Unit</code> --- and there are at least two non-defeq models with corners here: <code>𝓘(𝕜, Unit × Unit)</code> and <code>𝓘(𝕜, Unit).prod 𝓘(𝕜, Unit)</code>. This is the same issue as for products of normed spaces.<br>\nIn particular, there is no good way to create a (local) instance \"a discrete group is a 0-dimensional manifold\" as we cannot make a robust guess for the model with corners. Instead, avoiding the need to specify that by having <code>ContMDiffConstSMul</code> seems the way to go.</p>",
        "id": 609595933,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1783762104
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(Of course, we can and imho should have a constructor going from <code>ContMDiffConstSMul</code> w.r.t. a discrete group <code>G</code> to <code>ContMDiffSMul</code> (say, w.r.t. <code>𝓘(𝕜, Unit)</code>) --- but as a theorem, not an instance. (Perhaps this theorem should be a little more general, and take a type <code>X</code> with <code>Unique X</code> as argument --- and prove <code>ContMDiffSMul</code> w.r.t. <code>𝓘(𝕜, X)</code>. To be discussed!)</p>",
        "id": 609595934,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1783762108
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Whilst I agree with the conclusion (\"Sébastien is right\"), I do slightly worry about these issues in the manifold library in general (although I am not a big manifold user). We seem to have no canonical way of saying \"let X be an n-dimensional manifold\" which has the property that, for example, the product of two 2-dimensional manifolds is a 4-dimensional manifold. I don't know how much of an issue this will be in practice though. </p>\n<p>I think a good future project would be making Grassmannians as manifolds -- for example the space of all planes in <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}^4</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">4</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>. If my understanding is correct, there is no way of writing down any kind of canonical model space for such objects (there is a \"canonical\" tangent space at each point, but it moves with the point) so we are going to have to make some kind of symmetry-breaking choice and I don't really understand what the consequences of this will be (and I worry that they will be painful).</p>",
        "id": 609606274,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1783773422
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110038\">Kevin Buzzard</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Typeclasses.20for.20smooth.20actions.3A.20.60Cont.28M.29Diff.28Const.29SMul.60/near/609606274\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Whilst I agree with the conclusion (\"Sébastien is right\"), I do slightly worry about these issues in the manifold library in general (although I am not a big manifold user). We seem to have no canonical way of saying \"let X be an n-dimensional manifold\" which has the property that, for example, the product of two 2-dimensional manifolds is a 4-dimensional manifold.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes we do. It looks like: let <code>E</code> be a normed real vector space, assume that <code>E</code> has real dimension 4, let <code>M</code> be a manifold modelled on <code>E</code>. Then ... (insert here whatever theorem you prove on 4-dimensional manifolds).</p>\n<p>Just like it's better to work with <code>IsLocalization</code> than working with a specific model of a localization.</p>",
        "id": 609606808,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1783774123
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Aha I see. So we have a completely reasonable predicate \"I am 4-dimensional\", and indeed we will be able to prove that if X and Y are 2-dimesional then X x Y is 4-dimensional. My mistake was that I was imagining that we would have to choose one local model for all 4-d manifolds.</p>\n<p>I've moved the Grassmannian discussion to the Grassmannian thread.</p>",
        "id": 609607285,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1783774755
    }
]